entry.summary:
Astronomers found what may be a new dwarf planet in data from the Vera C. Rubin Observatory collected earlier this year. The object, dubbed ED138, orbits the sun beyond the reaches of Neptune and Pluto.&nbsp;
The Vera C. Rubin Observatory’s detection of a possible dwarf planet is just the start of many more discoveries in our cosmic neighborhood.&nbsp;
Around 4.3 billion miles from the sun, hiding beyond the orbit of Neptune, a mysterious world was spotted flitting about in the darkness by the omnipotent Vera C. Rubin Observatory. This orb is very likely to be the solar system’s newest dwarf planet—a large, round object that doesn’t quite fit the official definition of a full-blown planet.
Astronomers announced the discovery of this cosmic island—which, for now, is referred to as 2026 ED13…
